WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:01.200
Good morning John.

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.460
Guess what's behind this thing?

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.060
New York City!

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.370
And guess what's behind this thing?

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.370
It's me.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:10.370
I'm at home again.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:13.799
I was in New York because SciShow just got
a big new sponsor, and we we doing press tours,

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:17.910
talking to people, anyone who would listen
to us about science education and stuff.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.570
You may have noticed that we've never, ever
done a branded video here on Vlogbrothers-

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:21.619
this is not one of those.

00:00:21.619 --> 00:00:23.140
They don't know that I'm making this video.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.721
Emerson, for the most part, makes stuff that
you would never buy, unless you, like, own

00:00:25.721 --> 00:00:27.999
a power plant, or a Fortune 500 company, or
something.

00:00:27.999 --> 00:00:31.910
And so the advertisements we're doing together
aren't about getting people to buy their products,

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:33.360
because that's not the problem they have.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.820
The problem they have is that they solve big
engineering problems and big science problems

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.750
and to do that they need scientists and engineers.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:44.470
And with not as many people graduating with
so-called 'STEM degrees,' and more people

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:48.960
going to work for big new companies that you've
heard of like Google and and SpaceX and Tesla,

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:49.960
they're a little worried.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.230
And I share their worry, though for separate
reasons.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:56.290
I don't hire engineer people- for the most
part, I hire humanities people.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:58.540
Like writers, and video editors, and artists,
and stuff.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.640
I'm concerned because scientists and engineers
solve a lot of the big problems that we need

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.570
to solve, like how do we get clean water to
more people?

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:10.680
And how do we continue to power this amazing
lifestyle that I get to lead without destroying

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:11.680
the world?

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.440
So yeah, when this came to SciShow, I was
like, "Oh, this makes sense, actually.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:15.640
We will do that."

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:19.560
That STEM toolkit is a very valuable one,
and one that is being developed less often

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:20.560
by people.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.520
And what I kept being asked by people on TV
and radio was "Why?

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.030
Why are fewer people doing this?"

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:27.580
And I'm not an expert on that; I went through
school one time.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:28.810
That's pretty much all my data.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:32.150
But as with most things, my guess is it's
a lot of different reasons.

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:35.590
And one reason is that I think it's really
hard, and we just kind of sugarcoat it sometimes,

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:38.560
but it is hard, and that's huge amount of
information to stick into your brain.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.920
But I think, more than that, we're also told
that there's a certain type of person who

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.141
becomes a scientist or an engineer, and they're
just good at it.

00:01:45.141 --> 00:01:48.560
They're just good at math, and they're good
at science, and just engineering geniuses!

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.190
And that's a really dangerous myth, because
everyone I know who went through a science

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.830
or engineering degree, it was really hard
for them.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:56.830
It was really hard for me.

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:58.180
Nobody is born being good at math.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:02.440
I think some people are born really liking
that challenge.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:05.570
That's the thing that all of the scientists
I know have in common.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:08.090
Like, there's no other common trait amongst
them.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.909
They're mothers and rock climbers and punk
rockers.

00:02:10.909 --> 00:02:14.120
Some are into politics, or sports, or spend
their evenings on Tumblr.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.970
The only thing they have in common is that
passion for solving hard problems.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.870
And there are certainly non-science problems
that need to be solved as well.

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:24.409
That's why I hire lots of artists and animators
and video editors and stuff.

00:02:24.409 --> 00:02:27.840
I think often when we tell people to get into
STEM careers, it's not because it's interesting

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.239
or fascinating or cool, it's because that's
the best way to get a good job, so do that.

00:02:32.239 --> 00:02:35.930
If you don't do this terribly difficult thing,
then you will never get a good job.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:40.379
And I think that's a terrible thing to do
to a child, who is like fifteen years old.

00:02:40.379 --> 00:02:44.579
They're in high school, and you're telling
them, "Okay, just start panicking now.

00:02:44.579 --> 00:02:47.709
A decade before you will be done learning
all of these things."

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:48.709
That's-that's terrifying!

00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:52.629
We can't we, instead, treat this like every
other big problem and take it one step at

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:57.769
a time, being driven by not the end goal,
but by, you know, interests, and fascinations

00:02:57.769 --> 00:02:58.769
along the way.

00:02:58.769 --> 00:03:02.969
And I wanna say the most interesting toolkit
you can have is one that includes both and

00:03:02.969 --> 00:03:05.209
technology, engineering and liberal arts.

00:03:05.209 --> 00:03:08.640
That's the education I had, and I'm so grateful
for it.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.329
Anyway, this really is a career path that's
open to everyone.

00:03:11.329 --> 00:03:13.870
So that's why I was in New York; I was doing
things with them.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:17.370
And seeing lots of my friends and hanging
out with cool people, that was fun too.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.609
The advertisement we made together, just encouraging
people to be interested and fascinated by

00:03:20.609 --> 00:03:22.269
science, is running now.

00:03:22.269 --> 00:03:23.669
There's also a link below -- you can see it
on YouTube.

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:26.010
And if you're thinking to yourself, "Hank,
you were in New York City and you did not

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:29.569
tell me and I did not get to see you that
is so uncool," I was very busy.

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:31.090
But also, I will be back!

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.249
In April!

00:03:32.249 --> 00:03:37.170
With Harry and the Potters, Driftless Pony
Club, Andrew Huang, Rob Scallon, playing music

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:40.269
all, from like Chicago, all the way to New
York, there's like eight stops.

00:03:40.269 --> 00:03:42.730
You can check out more information in the
link in the description.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:44.379
Tour Because Awesome: East Coast!

00:03:44.379 --> 00:03:45.819
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

